WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.580
Nerf John Wick is basically a long intense gun fight

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.680
The intensity we were able to achieve partially comes from the sound design, and that's what I wanna talk about

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.620
I wanna show you how I created the sounds for these nerf blasters in this video

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:41.240
I need to make them as intense as real gunshots. So, I'm gonna have to follow one of the key rules of sound design

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:49.600
It all comes down to the layers and mixing of multiple sounds to create the illusion of a single intense sound.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:01:00.000
Now, hopefully after this video you too will be convinced that you don't need the most realistic weapons to make an awesome fight scene for your next project.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.480
All the gear and software that I use to record and mix Nerf John Wick are in the description below

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.480
A disclaimer, I do not have a degree in sound design. This is not a sponsored video in any way.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.600
I learned how to do this by doing it. Let's go

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.240
And that's the video. Thanks for watching !

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.040
Just kidding !

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.860
Ok, let's look deeper into that scene.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.640
First, I'm gonna mute some sounds.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.440
So that way you know exactly the sounds that have being used.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.940
Basically, that's a recording of me firing a Nerf gun,

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:58.560
and repeating that sound effect every time Niko fires the gun.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.720
For the darts hitting everybody, I've recorded me flicking a Nerf dart with my finger.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.200
I added some plastic foley sounds from a Nerf gun I recorded, right before the shots start firing

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.380
Let's mix that all together quick and see what we have so far.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.180
The sound is great, but it just sounds like a normal Nerf Blaster.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.300
There is no strength, no velocity, no true impact.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.480
So now, I'm gonna add some design elements,

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.180
to really amp it up.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.900
First, I'm gonna add some punch sounds.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.900
Can't forget to add a solid slap, to really hit a comic punchline.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.620
And a much more unique dart sound that has a tail on it to really hit that punch.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.280
Now that we've given some strength to the darts, we need to make them more dense

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.240
So what I'm going to do is make sure that we have some solid falling sounds, when the goons hit the ground.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.140
Next, we gonna add speed.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.060
I have two types of sounds that I'm specifically gonna add for this.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.880
First, "whooshes".

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.520
Some solid PVC "whooshes" that I've got from the FilmRiot Sound Pack.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.780
I love these things.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.460
Then I'll mix in a pre-designed bullet sound,

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.580
that implies the idea of a dangerous gun shot.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.480
Now, let's listen to all of them, mixed together and balanced.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.860
As you can hear, reality isn't enough.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:51.600
It's important to experiment with layering different sounds which can create more depth and complexity.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.360
Sometime, sound design is not about perfectly recreating a sound,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.500
but instead communicating a idea.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.080
Experiment is key, there is no one way to make a sound.

